{"answer": "ADR-045 sets the convention. A re:cinq npm package is published by a GitHub Actions release workflow. The workflow runs on `push` of tags matching `v*`, and can also be started with `workflow_dispatch`. It uses `permissions: contents: read` plus `id-token: write`. It first runs `npm install -g npm@^11.5.1`, because OIDC trusted publishing needs npm 11.5.1 or newer and Node 22 ships an older npm. It then runs `npm publish --provenance --access public`. Auth is npm Trusted Publishing over OIDC: a trusted publisher is registered on npmjs.com for the package and points at the repo and release workflow, so no `NPM_TOKEN` secret is used. A package name that has never been published can't have a trusted publisher yet, so its first publish is done once with a short-lived token. After that the trusted publisher is registered and the token is revoked. Published packages must also follow the standard manifest shape: `@re-cinq` scope, Apache-2.0 license, ESM-only, `files: [dist]`, an `exports` map, `publishConfig.access: public`, and a `repository` object. New workflows must also pin third-party actions by commit SHA, use `persist-credentials: false`, run `npm ci --ignore-scripts`, and scope the `GITHUB_TOKEN` permissions to the job. A repo with a private source repo, such as bowman-ui, omits `--provenance`, because provenance needs a public source repo.\n\nThe release job compares the git tag with the committed `package.json` version first, and fails before publishing if they disagree. The reason is a past incident. Tags `v0.5.0` through `v0.7.0` published nothing, and npm stayed on `0.3.0` for nine weeks. npm's rejection of a duplicate version looks like an auth failure, so nobody investigated (ai-agent-subsystem#139, `check-contracts-version.sh`). The guard turns that silent failure into a red job that names the fix.", "sources": ["adrs/ADR-045-npm-package-publishing.md"]}
```

